email = form["email"]
sql = "SELECT user_id, email, name FROM users WHERE email = ''" + email + "''"
rows = db.execute(sql).fetchall()

Someone types this into the search box:

x'' OR ''1''=''1

Statement: "The query returns every row of users, because the input closes the string literal and adds a condition that is true for every row."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. After concatenation the database receives WHERE email = ''x'' OR ''1''=''1''. The first quote of the input ends the literal, and ''1''=''1'' is true for every row, so the OR makes the whole condition true. The database cannot tell which part of the text was meant as data. With a bound parameter - WHERE email = :email and {"email": email} - the value travels separately from the SQL text and is compared as one plain string, so the same input matches no row. A common misconception is that escaping quotes by hand, or "only staff use this form", is enough; parameters are the fix, and they also cover every input you did not think of.', 1, 5
