DELETE FROM enrollments WHERE user_id = :user_id AND course_id = :course_id;

After:

UPDATE enrollments SET status = ''cancelled''
WHERE user_id = :user_id AND course_id = :course_id AND status = ''active'';

The course page was not changed. It still counts learners with:

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enrollments WHERE course_id = :course_id;

Statement: "After the switch, this query still returns the number of learners currently enrolled in the course."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. With the hard delete, a cancelled enrollment disappeared, so COUNT(*) only saw current learners. With the soft delete the row stays in the table with status ''cancelled'', and this query counts it. Every read that means "current" must now add AND status <> ''cancelled'' (or read from a view that does). That is the price of soft delete: the history is kept, but each query has to remember to hide it. Verify by running the count before and after cancelling one test enrollment: it should go down by one.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_211') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
