WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_221'), 'Course 1 "SQL & Data Modeling" has 4 enrollments (all active) and 6 lessons.

SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM courses c
JOIN enrollments e ON e.course_id = c.course_id
JOIN lessons l     ON l.course_id = c.course_id
WHERE c.course_id = 1;

Statement: "The query returns 24, because each of the 4 enrollments is paired with each of the 6 lessons of the course."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Both joins match on course_id only, and enrollments and lessons are two unrelated one-to-many children of the same course. A join returns one row for every combination of matching rows, so the 4 enrollments and the 6 lessons combine into 4 x 6 = 24 rows. Each row is now a (learner, lesson) pair, not a learner and not a lesson. This is the fan-out that inflates counts when you aggregate after such a join. Check it by running the join without COUNT and reading the rows, or by comparing with SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enrollments WHERE course_id = 1.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_221') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
