    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_221'), 'courses: 1 "SQL & Data Modeling", 2 "Web Application Fundamentals", 4 "Git for Teams".
enrollments: course 1 has 2 active rows, course 2 has 1 active row, course 4 has no rows yet.

SELECT c.course_id, COUNT(e.user_id) AS active_learners
FROM courses c
LEFT JOIN enrollments e ON e.course_id = c.course_id
WHERE e.status = ''active''
GROUP BY c.course_id
ORDER BY c.course_id;

Statement: "Git for Teams appears with 0 active learners, because a LEFT JOIN keeps every row of courses."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The LEFT JOIN does keep Git for Teams, with NULL in every enrollments column. But WHERE runs after the join, and for that row e.status = ''active'' compares NULL with ''active'', which gives NULL, not true, so the row is removed. A filter on the right table in WHERE turns a LEFT JOIN back into an inner join. Move the condition into the join - LEFT JOIN enrollments e ON e.course_id = c.course_id AND e.status = ''active'' - and Git for Teams comes back as (4, 0). Verify by checking that the report has as many rows as SELECT COUNT(*) FROM courses.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_221') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
