       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_sdm_222'), 2, 'Look at each join in turn. Which ones can remove a course or a learner completely (an inner join needs a match), and which ones repeat rows (enrollments and lesson_progress are both many rows per course)? Then check where the status filter sits: in WHERE or in the join condition.'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_sdm_222'), 3, 'A similar report per user: count each user''s enrollments and finished lessons without multiplying them. Aggregate each table on its own first - SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM enrollments GROUP BY user_id, and the same for finished lesson_progress rows - so each subquery has one row per user, then LEFT JOIN both subqueries to users and wrap each count in COALESCE(..., 0).');
