WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_311'), 'The schema was applied once from schema.sql:

CREATE TABLE enrollments (
    user_id     INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES users (user_id),
    course_id   INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES courses (course_id) ON DELETE RESTRICT,
    enrolled_at TEXT NOT NULL,
    status      TEXT NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, course_id)
);

Course 10 still has three enrollments. An admin script runs:

import sqlite3
db = sqlite3.connect("codedev.db")
db.execute("DELETE FROM courses WHERE course_id = 10")
db.commit()

Statement: "The DELETE fails with FOREIGN KEY constraint failed, because the schema declares ON DELETE RESTRICT."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. In SQLite, foreign key enforcement is off by default and must be turned on for each connection with PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON (unless the library was compiled with foreign keys on by default, which the standard builds are not). The script never runs the pragma, so the DELETE succeeds and the three enrollments now point to a course that no longer exists. The REFERENCES clause is stored in the schema either way, which is why it looks protected. PRAGMA foreign_key_check lists the orphan rows afterwards. PostgreSQL always enforces foreign keys, so the same script there would fail.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
