WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_341'), 'On a copy of production (SQLite, 3.2 million enrollments, busy_timeout = 5000 ms), the backfill

UPDATE enrollments SET state = status;

ran for 41 s. During it, POST /enroll failed with "database is locked".

The new backfill runs this statement in a loop, each run in its own transaction, with a 50 ms pause between runs, until it changes 0 rows (each run takes about 15 ms):

UPDATE enrollments SET state = status
WHERE rowid IN (SELECT rowid FROM enrollments WHERE state IS NULL LIMIT 1000);

Statement: "Batching this way shortens how long each write lock is held, so enroll requests can get the lock between batches instead of failing after 5,000 ms."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. SQLite allows one writer at a time, and a single UPDATE is a single transaction, so the one-statement backfill held the write lock for all 41 seconds and other writers gave up when their 5,000 ms busy timeout ran out. Each batch commits after about 15 ms, and the pause leaves the lock free, so a waiting enroll request gets its turn quickly. The batched version is also idempotent because it only touches rows where state is still NULL. A common misconception is that a backfill is harmless because it only writes to a new column; the lock covers the whole database, not one column.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_341') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
