ALTER TABLE enrollments ADD COLUMN source TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT ''web'';

Afterwards, SELECT source FROM enrollments returns ''web'' for every existing row.

Statement: "To run this statement, SQLite writes ''web'' into each of the 3.2 million existing rows, so it holds the write lock about as long as a full-table UPDATE would."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. SQLite''s ADD COLUMN only changes the table definition stored in the schema; existing rows are not rewritten, and when a row has no stored value for the new column SQLite returns the column''s default. That is why the statement takes about the same time on a small or a large table. The non-NULL constant default is what makes NOT NULL acceptable here. PostgreSQL 11 and later behaves similarly for constant defaults, while a volatile default rewrites the table. The misconception is to treat every schema change as a full-table rewrite; the slow parts are backfills, index builds and table rebuilds.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_341') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_341'), 'Expand-contract plan for renaming enrollments.status to state:

1. Add column state (nullable)
2. Deploy v42: writes status and state, reads status
3. Backfill state in batches
4. Deploy v43: reads state, writes both
5. Deploy v44: writes only state
6. Back up, then ALTER TABLE enrollments DROP COLUMN status

One day after step 6, v44 shows a bug.

Statement: "Redeploying v42 is a safe rollback here, because v42 already worked with this schema during the expand phase."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. v42 reads status, and step 6 dropped that column, so every v42 request that reads enrollments would fail with "no such column: status" - the same failure as the original incident. Even before step 6, v42 stopped being a safe target at step 5, when status stopped being written and went stale. After the contract step, the only way back is restoring the backup taken just before it (and losing later writes), which is why the drop comes last, after a waiting period. v43 is not a way back either, because it still writes status. The safe move here is to fix forward with a v45.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_sdm_341') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
